I’ll be reading from my essay, titled “Your House Must Pay: Racialized Violence and Exploitation in the South and the Ghostly Folklore that Followed.” 
Throughout this paper, I will be tracing a timeline of racialized violence and exploitation in the Southern United States, paying attention to how folklore has been developed and used to deny the Black community justice and recognition. I will also be using the ideas of DiAngelo’s White Fra-gility to offer possible psychological reasoning for why white people may feel comfortable mytholo-gizing the tragedies of the past to cope with transgenerational guilt and shame, which then, perpetu-ates the same structures of anti-Blackness, before coming to the suggestion that the United States must ameliorate itself by demonstrating radical acceptance and transparency. 
